### Task 3 — Timesheet Report Tab

**What:** Build the Timesheet Report tab content.

**Filters:**
- `date_from` (DatePicker — REQUIRED, no default, user must select)
- `date_to` (DatePicker — REQUIRED, no default, user must select)
- Employee (search select — optional)
- Project (search select — optional)
- "Generate Report" button — fetches data only when clicked (not on filter change, since date range is required)

**Display:**
- Grouped by employee, then by day, then sessions per day
- **Employee section:** Collapsible card or accordion for each employee
  - Header: Employee name, total regular hours (HH:MM), total overtime hours (HH:MM)
  - **Day rows:** Date (formatted as Mon, Jan 1), session list with clock-in time, clock-out time, duration
  - Each session row: Clock In (HH:MM AM/PM), Clock Out (HH:MM AM/PM), Duration (HH:MM), Project
- **Summary totals** at bottom: Grand total regular hours, grand total overtime hours across all employees
- All hours formatted as HH:MM using the `formatMinutesToHHMM` utility from Sprint 10

**Empty state:** "No timesheet data for the selected period" when the report returns empty

**Loading state:** Show `LoadingSpinner` while report is being generated

---

### Task 4 — Payroll Report Tab

**What:** Build the Payroll Report tab content.

**Filters:**
- `date_from` (DatePicker — REQUIRED)
- `date_to` (DatePicker — REQUIRED)
- Employee (search select — optional)
- "Generate Report" button

**Summary cards** (displayed above the table after report is generated):
- Total Employees (count)
- Total Regular Hours (HH:MM)
- Total Overtime Hours (HH:MM)
- Total Regular Pay ($X,XXX.XX)
- Total Overtime Pay ($X,XXX.XX)
- Total Pay ($X,XXX.XX)

Use `Card` components for each summary metric.

**Per-employee table:**
- Columns: Name, Email, Hourly Rate ($XX.XX), Regular Hours (HH:MM), OT Hours (HH:MM), OT Multiplier (e.g., 1.5x), Regular Pay ($X,XXX.XX), OT Pay ($X,XXX.XX), Total Pay ($X,XXX.XX), Sessions (count), Flagged (count), Manual Edits (count)
- All monetary values formatted as USD: `$X,XXX.XX` using `Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', { style: 'currency', currency: 'USD' })`
- All hour values formatted as HH:MM
- Flagged count > 0: show as red text or red badge
- Manual edits count > 0: show pencil icon with count

**"Export CSV" button:**
- Placed in the toolbar area next to the "Generate Report" button
- Only enabled after a report has been generated
- Triggers the CSV download flow (see Task 5)

---

### Task 5 — CSV Download Implementation

**What:** Implement the payroll CSV export functionality.

```typescript
const exportPayrollCSV = async (params: { date_from: string; date_to: string; employee_id?: string }) => {
  try {
    const response = await apiClient.get('/time-clock/reports/payroll/export', {
      params,
      responseType: 'blob',
    });

    const url = window.URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([response.data]));
    const link = document.createElement('a');
    link.href = url;
    link.download = `payroll_${params.date_from}_${params.date_to}.csv`;
    document.body.appendChild(link);
    link.click();
    document.body.removeChild(link);
    window.URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
  } catch (error) {
    // Show error toast
    toast.error('Failed to export payroll CSV');
  }
};
```

**Requirements:**
- Use the same `apiClient` (axios instance) used by the rest of the app for authentication headers
- Set `responseType: 'blob'` to handle binary response
- Create Blob from response data
- Trigger download with filename format: `payroll_YYYY-MM-DD_YYYY-MM-DD.csv`
- Clean up the Object URL after download triggers
- Show loading state on the Export button while downloading
- Show error toast if export fails

**Do NOT:**
- Open a new browser tab for the download
- Use `window.open()` — the auth token won't be sent
- Hardcode the API URL — use the configured `apiClient`

